{% set page_eyebrow = "Platform architecture" %}
{% set page_title = "Ports, adapters, and a boundary that is actually checked" %}
{% set page_lede = "Enough of the technical shape to judge whether the platform stays testable as it grows. Implementation detail lives in the linked design documents." %}
{% set page_trail = [{"label": "Architecture"}] %}
{% set page_sections = [
  {"id": "shape", "title": "The shape"},
  {"id": "boundary", "title": "The boundary rule"},
  {"id": "components", "title": "Component catalogue"},
  {"id": "request", "title": "A request through the system"},
  {"id": "platform", "title": "Infrastructure"}
] %}
{% extends "pages/_page.jinja" %}
{% from "data/architecture.jinja" import components, boundary_rule, ownership %}

{% block sections %}
  {{ ui.section_open(1, "shape", "The shape",
     "Three processes, one database, one message broker. The conceptual picture first.") }}
    <p>
      An HTTP service accepts requests and returns canonical state. Work that
      outlives a request is handed to a worker over a queue. Generation runs
      inside a state graph that can suspend for as long as an editorial review
      takes and resume where it stopped. Everything durable lives in
      PostgreSQL.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing in that description is unusual, which is the intent. The design
      choices worth examining are where the boundaries fall and what enforces
      them.
    </p>
    {{ ui.photo(
      "patchbay",
      "The back of a studio equipment rack, showing two rows of patch-bay jacks and cable looms dressed away to one side.",
      "Ports and adapters, in the older sense. What matters is that every connection is named and reachable.") }}
  {{ ui.section_close() }}

  {{ ui.section_open(2, "boundary", "The boundary rule",
     "Hexagonal architecture is a claim many projects make. This one is testable.") }}
    <blockquote class="rule">
      <p>{{ boundary_rule.statement }}</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>{{ boundary_rule.enforcement }}</p>
    <p>
      That distinction matters more than the pattern. An import rule that only
      exists in a contributing guide degrades on a deadline; one that fails the
      build does not. The check runs as part of the standard lint target, so a
      violation stops a change before review rather than during it.
    </p>
    {{ ui.evidence(boundary_rule.evidence) }}
  {{ ui.section_close() }}

  {{ ui.section_open(3, "components", "Component catalogue",
     "Responsibility, adapter, and where each component keeps state.") }}
    {{ rec.component_catalogue(components) }}
  {{ ui.section_close() }}

  {{ ui.section_open(4, "request", "A request through the system",
     "The source-to-script path, as an ordered sequence. No diagram required.") }}
    <ol class="sequence">
      <li>A client posts a source document with an idempotency key. The upload is stored and recorded.</li>
      <li>The client creates an ingestion job and attaches the upload or a remote source URI.</li>
      <li>Authorization middleware resolves the principal before any resource runs.</li>
      <li>The job is accepted; normalization runs as worker tasks off the request path.</li>
      <li>Sources are classified, weighted, and merged into a canonical TEI episode with provenance in its header.</li>
      <li>The client polls the job until the source context is ready.</li>
      <li>Generation composes a structured brief with pinned reference revisions and guardrails, then runs the graph.</li>
      <li>Evaluators execute in parallel; findings aggregate and the graph routes on the result.</li>
      <li>The run suspends at the editorial checkpoint, holding durable state until a person decides.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="section__more">
      Steps one to six are implemented. Steps seven and eight are partly built.
      Step nine is designed.
      <a class="text-link" href="/episodic/workflow/">See the stage-by-stage maturity</a>
    </p>
  {{ ui.section_close() }}

  {{ ui.section_open(5, "platform", "Infrastructure",
     "DigitalOcean Kubernetes, reconciled by FluxCD. The application owns less of this than it might appear.") }}
    <p>
      OpenTofu provisions the clusters and shared services. FluxCD reconciles
      platform and application manifests from a GitOps repository. Postgres is
      supplied by the CloudNativePG operator, cache by the Valkey operator, and
      messaging by the RabbitMQ operator. Observability is Prometheus, Tempo,
      and Loki behind an OpenTelemetry Collector. Ingress is Traefik with
      cert-manager issuing certificates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Episodic supplies an image, a runtime contract, health semantics, and a
      Helm chart. The platform supplies everything else. That split is the
      subject of its own page, because conflating the two is how a working
      chart gets mistaken for a deployed environment.
    </p>
    {{ rec.contract_table(
      "Application and platform ownership",
      "architecture-ownership",
      ownership,
      ["concern", "owner", "detail"],
      ["Concern", "Owned by", "Detail"]) }}
    <p class="section__more">
      <a class="text-link" href="/episodic/hosting/">Read the Nile Valley hosting contract</a>
      ·
      <a class="text-link" href="/episodic/interfaces/">See which interfaces are live</a>
    </p>
  {{ ui.section_close() }}
{% endblock %}
